1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:05,130
Forty-nine years ago, humanity launched a machine toward the stars.

2
00:00:05,370 --> 00:00:10,889
Today, that machine, Voyager One, is about to reach a distance called one light-day.

3
00:00:11,129 --> 00:00:16,775
And once you understand what that means, you will never look at the night sky the same way.

4
00:00:17,015 --> 00:00:23,707
It left Earth carrying a golden record, a message in a bottle thrown into an ocean we barely understand.

5
00:00:24,557 --> 00:00:29,500
A light-day is the distance a beam of light travels in twenty-four hours.

6
00:00:29,740 --> 00:00:35,088
A light-year is that same journey, repeated three hundred and sixty-five times.

7
00:00:35,328 --> 00:00:40,185
One ray of light, running for an entire year, without ever slowing down.

8
00:00:40,425 --> 00:00:45,197
In a single day, light covers about twenty-six billion kilometers.

9
00:00:45,437 --> 00:00:52,236
That is the one-day distance Voyager is about to reach, and it took the spacecraft forty-nine years to fly it.

10
00:00:53,086 --> 00:00:56,045
Written as a number, it looks harmless.

11
00:00:56,285 --> 00:00:59,734
Nine point four six trillion kilometers.

12
00:01:00,194 --> 00:01:03,729
But your brain simply shrugs at a number like that.

13
00:01:03,969 --> 00:01:06,479
So let's do something about it.

14
00:01:06,719 --> 00:01:10,830
Let's shrink the universe until it fits on a map you already know.

15
00:01:11,070 --> 00:01:14,818
A number with thirteen digits, and no way to see it.

16
00:01:15,668 --> 00:01:21,443
Light is the fastest thing in the universe: three hundred thousand kilometers every second.

17
00:01:21,683 --> 00:01:26,711
In a single second, a beam of light could circle the Earth seven and a half times.

18
00:01:26,951 --> 00:01:30,741
Seven and a half laps, before you finish saying the word one.

19
00:01:31,811 --> 00:01:34,450
Now keep that beam going.

20
00:01:34,870 --> 00:01:36,143
Days pass.

21
00:01:36,563 --> 00:01:37,493
Weeks.

22
00:01:37,913 --> 00:01:39,079
Seasons.

23
00:01:39,499 --> 00:01:46,106
After one full year of travel, that single ray has covered nine point four six trillion kilometers.

24
00:01:46,346 --> 00:01:51,715
Spring, summer, autumn and winter pass on Earth, while that ray is still racing outward.

25
00:01:51,955 --> 00:01:56,493
Same speed, day after day, with no stops and no slowing down.

26
00:01:56,733 --> 00:02:00,459
And after all that, it has covered just one light-year.

27
00:02:01,309 --> 00:02:03,436
You can say that number.

28
00:02:03,856 --> 00:02:05,727
You just can't feel it.

29
00:02:06,147 --> 00:02:08,593
So let's bring it down to Earth.

30
00:02:08,833 --> 00:02:10,043
Literally.

31
00:02:10,463 --> 00:02:14,851
Nine point four six trillion is a perfectly valid number.

32
00:02:15,091 --> 00:02:21,697
It just means nothing to us, because we have never walked, driven or flown anything remotely that far.

33
00:02:21,937 --> 00:02:27,392
Our minds evolved for distances we can see: a hill, a river, a day's walk.

34
00:02:27,632 --> 00:02:30,271
So we need a different kind of ruler.

35
00:02:31,121 --> 00:02:35,167
Picture the Praça da Sé, in the heart of São Paulo.

36
00:02:35,407 --> 00:02:38,601
In the middle of the square, we place a football.

37
00:02:38,841 --> 00:02:41,309
That ball is the Sun.

38
00:02:41,729 --> 00:02:46,522
The real Sun is almost one point four million kilometers wide.

39
00:02:46,762 --> 00:02:49,955
Here, it is twenty-two centimeters.

40
00:02:50,375 --> 00:02:56,129
Everything else in the Solar System will be scaled down by exactly the same amount.

41
00:02:56,369 --> 00:03:03,146
Place it on the ground, and the Sun, the engine of all life on Earth, becomes something you could pick up with both hands.

42
00:03:04,216 --> 00:03:06,364
Now, where is the Earth?

43
00:03:07,084 --> 00:03:11,472
It is a speck two millimeters wide, about the size of a pinhead.

44
00:03:11,712 --> 00:03:16,399
And it sits twenty-four meters away from the ball, roughly thirty paces.

45
00:03:16,639 --> 00:03:20,216
Everything you have ever known fits on that dot.

46
00:03:20,456 --> 00:03:24,887
The Moon would be an even smaller grain, six centimeters from the Earth.

47
00:03:25,127 --> 00:03:29,707
Between the two, there is nothing: just empty space, almost all of it.

48
00:03:30,557 --> 00:03:33,387
Keep walking, and the planets appear.

49
00:03:33,627 --> 00:03:38,335
Jupiter, a small marble, one hundred and twenty meters from the ball.

50
00:03:38,575 --> 00:03:42,046
Saturn, two hundred and twenty-five meters.

51
00:03:42,286 --> 00:03:45,351
Uranus, nearly half a kilometer.

52
00:03:45,771 --> 00:03:50,714
And Neptune, the last planet, a little over seven hundred meters away.

53
00:03:50,954 --> 00:03:54,425
Whole worlds, separated by a few city blocks.

54
00:03:54,665 --> 00:03:58,903
Each of these worlds is a speck, drifting in a vast emptiness.

55
00:03:59,143 --> 00:04:04,897
Light, the fastest thing there is, needs about four hours to cross from the ball to Neptune.

56
00:04:05,747 --> 00:04:10,433
Beyond Neptune lies Pluto, and the icy debris of the Kuiper Belt.

57
00:04:10,673 --> 00:04:17,387
From above, the whole Solar System, every planet, every orbit, fits inside the center of São Paulo.

58
00:04:17,627 --> 00:04:22,612
Light needs about five and a half hours to travel from the ball to Pluto.

59
00:04:22,852 --> 00:04:28,435
Hold that number in mind: five and a half hours, across our entire planetary system.

60
00:04:29,505 --> 00:04:32,229
Now, where is Voyager One?

61
00:04:32,949 --> 00:04:39,427
It is the farthest machine humans have ever built, moving at sixty-one thousand kilometers per hour.

62
00:04:39,667 --> 00:04:44,909
It swept past Jupiter and Saturn long ago, and it has been heading out ever since.

63
00:04:45,149 --> 00:04:51,819
It was launched in nineteen seventy-seven, carrying a golden record with sounds and greetings from Earth.

64
00:04:52,059 --> 00:04:57,322
Today, it is the most distant human-made object, and it still sends data back.

65
00:04:58,172 --> 00:05:02,795
So, in our scale, how far has Voyager gone in forty-nine years?

66
00:05:03,335 --> 00:05:05,909
Only four kilometers.

67
00:05:06,329 --> 00:05:07,284
Four.

68
00:05:07,704 --> 00:05:16,913
After half a century of flight at sixty-one thousand kilometers per hour, the most distant machine ever built would still be inside the city of São Paulo.

69
00:05:17,153 --> 00:05:20,154
That is a walk of less than an hour.

70
00:05:20,394 --> 00:05:24,057
Forty-nine years of flight, for a few city blocks.

71
00:05:24,907 --> 00:05:27,140
And then the city ends.

72
00:05:27,560 --> 00:05:33,570
Beyond that, there is nothing left to pass: just an empty road, stretching toward the horizon.

73
00:05:33,810 --> 00:05:37,515
No more signs, no more towns, no more light.

74
00:05:37,755 --> 00:05:40,692
Just the dark, and the long distance.

75
00:05:40,932 --> 00:05:44,915
And we still have almost fifteen hundred kilometers to go.

76
00:05:45,765 --> 00:05:52,244
Even the Oort Cloud, the vast sphere of icy bodies wrapped around the Sun, begins much farther out.

77
00:05:52,484 --> 00:05:57,789
Voyager One would need about three hundred years just to reach its inner edge.

78
00:05:58,029 --> 00:06:01,436
And roughly thirty thousand years to cross it.

79
00:06:01,676 --> 00:06:08,816
Imagine a shell of frozen comets, billions of them, so far apart that you would never see one from another.

80
00:06:09,666 --> 00:06:11,494
So let's keep going.

81
00:06:11,914 --> 00:06:16,408
On this scale, one light-year is about fifteen hundred kilometers.

82
00:06:16,648 --> 00:06:27,884
We leave the square behind, cross the state of São Paulo, climb into the mountains of Minas Gerais, and keep following the golden line, over ridges and valleys, toward the dry backlands of Bahia.

83
00:06:28,124 --> 00:06:32,320
Even a modern jet would need about two hours for this stretch.

84
00:06:32,560 --> 00:06:37,588
For Voyager, on this scale, that distance will never end in our lifetimes.

85
00:06:38,438 --> 00:06:42,421
Finally the land opens up, and the Atlantic appears.

86
00:06:42,661 --> 00:06:45,172
Salvador, in Bahia.

87
00:06:45,592 --> 00:06:50,428
One thousand, four hundred and ninety-four kilometers from the football.

88
00:06:50,668 --> 00:06:54,352
That is the length of a single light-year, at our scale.

89
00:06:54,592 --> 00:06:58,340
From a football in a city square, to the sea in Bahia.

90
00:06:58,580 --> 00:07:03,245
And that is only one light-year, a single step on the cosmic map.

91
00:07:04,095 --> 00:07:07,118
From orbit, you can see it all at once.

92
00:07:07,358 --> 00:07:12,258
A single light-year, drawn from the Praça da Sé to the coast of Bahia.

93
00:07:12,498 --> 00:07:18,827
And Voyager One, after forty-nine years of flight, is still that tiny dot, inside São Paulo.

94
00:07:19,897 --> 00:07:22,941
Now let's return to the real scale.

95
00:07:23,181 --> 00:07:28,721
To fly across one light-year, at the speed of Voyager One, how long would you have to wait?

96
00:07:29,261 --> 00:07:32,860
On the map, a light-year looked like a long flight.

97
00:07:33,100 --> 00:07:40,602
In reality, it is nine trillion kilometers, and Voyager travels at about seventeen kilometers every second.

98
00:07:41,452 --> 00:07:45,094
Seventeen thousand, seven hundred years.

99
00:07:45,514 --> 00:07:48,664
Empires would rise and fall.

100
00:07:49,084 --> 00:07:52,256
Languages would be born and forgotten.

101
00:07:52,496 --> 00:07:56,095
Calendars would be reinvented again and again.

102
00:07:56,335 --> 00:08:00,339
And through all of it, the spacecraft would simply keep flying.

103
00:08:00,579 --> 00:08:05,351
That is how long you would have to wait, just to cross one single light-year.

104
00:08:06,201 --> 00:08:12,018
Seventeen thousand years ago, people were only beginning to spread across the Americas.

105
00:08:12,258 --> 00:08:18,844
About eleven thousand years ago, a young woman we call Luzia lived in what is now Minas Gerais.

106
00:08:19,084 --> 00:08:23,408
Her skeleton is among the oldest ever found in the Americas.

107
00:08:23,648 --> 00:08:29,764
And even her whole lifetime, and all the time since, would not be enough for Voyager to finish its trip.

108
00:08:30,614 --> 00:08:33,594
Pyramids rise in Egypt.

109
00:08:34,014 --> 00:08:37,740
Rome grows from a small city into an empire.

110
00:08:37,980 --> 00:08:43,691
Ships cross the Atlantic, and in the year fifteen hundred, the Portuguese reach Brazil.

111
00:08:43,931 --> 00:08:48,746
Thousands of years of human history, and Voyager is still on its way.

112
00:08:48,986 --> 00:08:56,552
In that time, sailors learned to navigate by the stars, and astronomers slowly learned how far away those stars really are.

113
00:08:57,402 --> 00:09:00,062
What if you travel by airplane?

114
00:09:00,602 --> 00:09:06,761
At nine hundred kilometers per hour, one light-year would take about one point two million years.

115
00:09:07,001 --> 00:09:10,536
A jet crosses Brazil in about three hours.

116
00:09:10,776 --> 00:09:15,889
This flight would last four times longer than the entire history of our species.

117
00:09:16,739 --> 00:09:20,487
What about a car, at a hundred kilometers per hour?

118
00:09:21,027 --> 00:09:23,260
Eleven million years.

119
00:09:23,680 --> 00:09:27,642
Eleven million years ago, there were no humans on Earth.

120
00:09:27,882 --> 00:09:31,288
Our ancestors were still far in the future.

121
00:09:32,138 --> 00:09:36,718
And on foot, walking five kilometers an hour, without ever stopping?

122
00:09:37,258 --> 00:09:44,868
The landscape would change around you, again and again, as the ages roll by, and the rivers, mountains and forests are reshaped.

123
00:09:45,108 --> 00:09:46,338
No rest.

124
00:09:46,758 --> 00:09:48,053
No sleep.

125
00:09:48,473 --> 00:09:53,586
Just one step after another, for longer than most of life's history on land.

126
00:09:54,436 --> 00:09:57,544
Two hundred and sixteen million years.

127
00:09:57,784 --> 00:10:04,967
You would have started that walk in the age of the first dinosaurs, in the Triassic, and you would only be arriving now.

128
00:10:05,207 --> 00:10:08,656
That is how long a single light-year really is.

129
00:10:08,896 --> 00:10:15,354
Back then, South America and Africa were still joined in a single enormous landmass called Pangaea.

130
00:10:16,204 --> 00:10:19,013
And that is only the first step.

131
00:10:19,253 --> 00:10:23,108
The nearest star beyond the Sun is Alpha Centauri.

132
00:10:23,348 --> 00:10:33,304
On our scale, it lies more than six thousand kilometers from the football, as far as the distance from São Paulo to Cape Town, across the whole South Atlantic.

133
00:10:34,154 --> 00:10:42,574
Farther still, at the center of our galaxy, twenty-six thousand light-years away, lies a black hole four million times the mass of the Sun.

134
00:10:42,814 --> 00:10:45,986
Its name is Sagittarius A star.

135
00:10:46,226 --> 00:10:51,040
Stars whip around it in only sixteen years, bent by its gravity.

136
00:10:51,280 --> 00:10:57,866
And that is all still inside our own galaxy, the Milky Way, which holds hundreds of billions of stars.

137
00:10:58,716 --> 00:11:01,845
The universe is bigger than we imagine.

138
00:11:02,085 --> 00:11:03,508
Keep looking up.

139
00:11:03,928 --> 00:11:10,534
Tonight, when you look at the sky, remember: the light reaching your eyes began its journey long before you were born.

